# Generated by `make audit-metrics` — a local/CI analysis artifact, not a
# committed one: per-file line counts change on nearly every commit, so
# committing it would churn every PR. docs/AUDIT_BASELINE.json IS committed.
docs/AUDIT_METRICS.json
.audit-wip/

# Ansible deployment: real inventories and run artifacts (examples are tracked)
deploy/ansible/inventory/*.yml
!deploy/ansible/inventory/*.yml.example
deploy/ansible/ansible-deploy.log
deploy/ansible/.ansible_facts/
deploy/ansible/tests/preflight-tests.log

# The ADR KB blob is a BUILD ARTIFACT, not a source. `docs/adr/*.md` is the
# source of truth; `build-adr-kb` derives this from it. It was tracked until
# 2026-08, at ~57 MB per regeneration — every ADR edit wrote a fresh copy into
# history, and GitHub had started warning on the push.
#
# Nothing ever read the committed copy. `make install` depends on the `adr-kb`
# target and `release.yml` runs `build-adr-kb` before packaging, so both
# rebuild it first; `verify-adr-kb-sync` only diffs the .sha256 below and never
# opens the store. It was 57 MB of history for a file with no consumer.
#
# `assets/mae-adr.cozo.sha256` STAYS TRACKED and is deliberately not covered by
# this rule: the ADR-059 Phase E staleness gate fires on that checksum moving,
# so it must remain diffable.
#
# It is NOT a verification oracle, and must not be used as one. A sled store is
# rewritten in place the first time it is opened and is not byte-reproducible
# across builds, so a legitimately rebuilt — or merely once-opened — store
# hashes differently from the committed value (measured 2026-08: local d1ab9b…
# vs committed ec30dd…). It records "`make adr-kb` was re-run", nothing more.
# A downloaded artifact is verified against the release's SHA256SUMS instead.
#
# Build it with `make adr-kb`, or fetch it with `make fetch-adr-kb`.
assets/mae-adr.cozo
# No trailing slash on the four patterns above: a sqlite store is a FILE, not a
# directory (ADR-108), and `foo/` matches only directories -- so the built store
# stopped being ignored the moment RELEASE_ASSET_ENGINE flipped, and was one
# `git add -A` away from committing a binary database.

# The same reasoning applies to the other three bundled KBs (ADR-076), which
# #673 left tracked: ~58 MB of binary that is REBUILT, never read from git.
#
#   assets/mae-manual.cozo/db        39 MB
#   assets/mae-devpractices.cozo/db  18 MB
#   assets/mae-practices.cozo/db    784 KB
#
# The decisive point is the one recorded above for the ADR store: a sled store
# is rewritten in place the first time it is OPENED and is not byte-reproducible
# across builds. A tracked file that cannot be reproduced byte-for-byte dirties
# every contributor's working tree forever — `make install` rebuilds all four,
# so merely installing MAE left ~58 MB of unstageable churn behind, every time.
#
# Nothing reads them from the repo. Every consumer builds first: `install`
# depends on `manual-kb`/`practices-kb`/`devpractices-kb`, `install-manual` and
# friends depend on their own build target, and release.yml + ci.yml both run
# the `build-*-kb` binaries before copying. Verified before removing them.
#
# Their `.sha256` siblings go too, unlike the ADR one: no gate diffs them (the
# ADR-059 Phase E staleness gate is ADR-only), they are regenerated by the same
# build, and they churn for the same reason.
#
# Build with `make manual-kb` / `practices-kb` / `devpractices-kb`, or just
# `make install`, which does all of it.
#
# NOTE: this stops the BLEEDING. Reclaiming the existing history is #674 and
# needs a rewrite at a release boundary.
assets/mae-manual.cozo
assets/mae-manual.cozo.sha256
assets/mae-practices.cozo
assets/mae-practices.cozo.sha256
assets/mae-devpractices.cozo
assets/mae-devpractices.cozo.sha256
